[2026-10-19 20:29:20.232] [VERBOSE] streaming #1: RAW REQUEST BODY >>>{"model":"qwen3.5:4b","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.\n\n<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- meeting_rename: meeting_rename({ meetingDir, title }) — rename meeting to human-readable title\n- meeting_append_entry: meeting_append_entry({ meetingDir, speaker, phase, title?, summary, content }) — write full contribution to meeting blackboard\n- meeting_read_index: meeting_read_index({ meetingDir, limit? }) — list meeting entries\n- meeting_read_entry: meeting_read_entry({ meetingDir, entryPath }) — read full entry content\n- report_finding: Report one verified review finding (requires a trace and a refutation attempt)\n- get_context_remaining: Check remaining context tokens (get_context_remaining).\n- new_context: Start a fresh context window (new_context); save notes first.\n- notes: Persistent private notes across context windows (notes).\n- history: Read-only recovery of prior context windows (history).\n- web_search: Use for web research questions. Prefer {queries:[...]} with 2-4 varied angles over a single query for broader coverage. Omit provider unless explicitly overriding the configured default.\n- source_check: Gather structured source evidence and passage-level citations for manual semantic review of a claim.\n- fetch_content: Use to fetch URL content, direct images, GitHub repos, and videos.\n- get_search_content: Use after web_search, source_check, or fetch_content to retrieve stored content via responseId. Use findText to locate passages without paging through the full content.\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>\n\n<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Use meeting_append_entry to write your FULL brainstorm/debate contribution to disk. Then reply ONLY with 'WROTE_ENTRY: <one-sentence summary>' — do NOT repeat the full content in chat.\n- Report every code-review, audit, or bug-hunt finding by calling report_finding once per finding, instead of describing findings in prose.\n- Any defect-shaped statement in your answer must go through report_finding, whatever you call the section it sits in. \"Edge cases\", \"observations\", \"environmental notes\", \"caveats\", \"things to be aware of\" and \"not a code defect, but...\" are the same claim wearing a different label, and relabelling one does not exempt it. If it says something can fail, crash, raise, be wrong, or be mishandled, it is a finding.\n- If you cannot produce a trace for a candidate, call report_finding with confidence \"unverified\" and no severity — do not describe it in prose instead. Reporting nothing and then listing concerns in the summary is the one route that is never acceptable: it produces exactly the unlabelled, untraced claims this whole process exists to stop.\n- Any claim about language, library, or version behaviour belongs in report_finding with a verified_by command, wherever it appears — including in your final summary. Do not state a version bound in prose that you have not checked.\n- Before calling report_finding, look for the guard, early return, signal handler, or caller-side filter that would defeat the failure — checking a few lines either side of the code you quoted first — and record that search in the refutation fields.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>\n\n<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.3.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.3.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.3.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>\n\n<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/chenxigui/develop/LlamaServer/AGENTS.md\">\n# LlamaServer — compact reference for agents\n\n**The models and frameworks this server rests on — with the upstream links to\nread (model card, llama.cpp, Telegraph, XcodeGen), how pi discovers the docs,\nand how to point a client at the server: [`docs/STACK.md`](docs/STACK.md).**\n\n## Build & test (CI only)\n\nNo local Xcode required. Everything runs in GitHub Actions:\n\n1. Commit and push to `main`/`master` (or open a PR).\n2. CI pipeline `.github/workflows/build.yml` on an `xcode-27` runner (macOS 27 arm64, Xcode 27 → iOS 27 SDK) runs two jobs:\n   - **test**: `xcodegen generate` → `xcodebuild test` (simulator, unit tests only; destination `iPhone 17` — Xcode 27 images have no iPhone 16 device type)\n   - **build**: `xcodegen generate` → `xcodebuild` (unsigned Release) → `bash scripts/package-ipa.sh` → upload artifact.\n3. Check the \"Actions\" tab for results. The test job must pass; the build job produces `LlamaServer-unsigned.ipa`.\n\n## CI notes\n\n- `vendor/llama.xcframework` is cached across runs by the release tag + script hash. Only re-downloaded when the tag or `fetch-llama-xcframework.sh` changes.\n- **No Package.swift.** Project is defined in `project.yml` (XcodeGen). Always edit `project.yml`, never the `.xcodeproj` directly.\n- **No local llama.cpp compilation.** `vendor/llama.xcframework` is prebuilt, fetched with `bash scripts/fetch-llama-xcframework.sh`. Pinned to tag `b11557`.\n- The `.xcodeproj` is **never committed** (`*.xcodeproj` in `.gitignore`). CI regenerates it on every run.\n\n## Debugging a deployed build\n\nThe app runs on device, so behaviour is diagnosed from its logs — there is no debugger attached and no local build to fall back on.\n\n- **Identify the build before concluding anything.** Every build reports itself: `system_fingerprint` on any chat/completions response is `llamaserver-<sha>`, and the model-load line says `Model loaded (context …, build llamaserver-<sha>)` (`LlamaServerBuildSHA`, injected by CI). A stale artifact and the current one are otherwise indistinguishable — this cost two redeploys on 2026-10-17.\n- **Pull the logs off the device over HTTP** (no cable, no Files app): `curl 'http://<device>:8443/logs?name=inference&tail=500'`. Full query (`name`, `tail`, `backups`, `format`) and the payload-file caveat are in the `GET /logs` gotcha below.\n- **The in-app panel is not the log.** It renders basic (error/warn/info) lines only, capped and truncated (`LogPanelSpec`); the three on-disk files are authoritative.\n- **Prompt/response payloads are opt-in**: they land in the payloads file only while \"log prompt & response content\" is enabled (which needs log level *Verbose*). Missing prompts are a toggle, not a bug.\n- **On-device compliance** is the `llm-compliance-test` skill (runs `llm-comply openai-chat` against a deployed build).\n- **Deploying a new build is a LAN push, not a cable**: `scripts/upload-ipa.sh --url http://<device>:8443` downloads the latest green `build.yml` artifact with `gh` and `PUT`s it to `GET|PUT /v1/updates[/ipa?build=<sha>]`; the app stores it in `Documents/updates/` (`LlamaServer-unsigned.ipa`, rotated `LlamaServer-unsigned.prev.ipa`, `latest.json`), the user taps the `.ipa` in Files, and AltStore installs it in place (models survive). Runbook: the `upload-ipa` skill. Three things to hold onto: the build id lives in `latest.json`/the log and **not** in the file name (Files sorts by name, so a hash-suffixed name hides the newest build); the routes exist **only while a model is loaded** (they ride the inference listener — no second port, no second permission prompt), so a build that cannot start a model cannot be replaced this way, and the escape hatch is picking a smaller model in the UI; and validation (`UpdateSpec.rejection`) runs before any filesystem call, so a refused upload — no `?build=`, over the 128 MB cap, no zip header — leaves both slots untouched. The cap is best-effort (Telegraph buffers the whole body before the handler runs; the real IPA is ~4 MB), and uploads are serialized by their own lock, never the inference queue.\n\n## Performance\n\n**Measured, not inferred — [`docs/PERFORMANCE.md`](docs/PERFORMANCE.md) is the baseline** (rig, method, per-phase numbers, and the claims the logs disprove). Read it before making or repeating any performance claim; add a row when you measure something new.\n\nHeadline numbers (A19 Pro, 4B Q4_K_M, ctx 131072, KV q8_0, MTP depth 1, build `470957a`):\n\n- **Prompt: 170–350 t/s** for genuinely new tokens. A cold 24k-token agent prompt costs ~80 s; prefix reuse is what makes turns cheap (`decoding 113 new` of 48,450), including across a context recreate.\n- **Generation: 10–17 t/s**, falling with context.\n- **`verify-gpu` is 80–90% of every MTP round** (51 ms/round at 0.4k context → 149 ms at 47k): the verify pass attends over the whole KV (843 MB at 48k) plus the weights. CPU/host work is ~3 ms/round — micro-optimising the Swift loop cannot move the needle.\n- **MTP depth 1 is a measured net win on generation, a loss on prefill** (paired A/B, byte-identical output): **+11–16% generation** at 0.04k–17.7k context, and **~4% slower prefill** in the thermal-bracket run (the first pair, taken straight off a long session, measured +6–11% / −9% — see `docs/PERFORMANCE.md` for both). The prefill cost is the MTP path also mirroring each prompt chunk into the draft context. For a pi-style turn (small delta prefill + a few hundred generated tokens at ~35k) the two roughly cancel.\n- **Snapshot round-trip ~1.2 s and ~700 MB of writes per turn** (see the snapshot gotcha below).\n- Thermals swing results ~30% (same 48k context: 148.81 vs 103.49 ms/round) — always quote context length, and pair A/B runs.\n\n## Architecture\n\n- **Single process.** llama.cpp linked as a framework (`import llama`), runs in-process. No separate server binary.\n- **HTTP server**: Telegraph framework. Intercepts streaming requests via `interceptHandler` on `StreamableServer` (bypasses normal route handler for SSE writes direct to `HTTPConnection`).\n- **Serial inference queue**: `DispatchQueue(label: \"llama.inference.serial\")` — all `LlamaInference` calls go through this queue (not thread-safe).\n- **Context recreated** every 10 generations (`contextRecreateThreshold`) to flush Metal GPU/allocator state that can cause hangs. The recreate runs OFF the response path (`RecreateTracker`, called after the finish chunk in streaming), and the prompt-prefix reuse snapshot survives it (`ReuseSurvival`) — a dense model's `cachedTokenIds` and the on-disk seq blob are preserved so the next turn restores from disk instead of re-decoding the whole prompt.\n- **Non-streaming** chat completions: synchronous on the serial queue, returns full response once done.\n- **Streaming** chat completions: async on the serial queue, each token flushed as SSE via direct connection write. Keep-alive sends empty delta chunks every 2s.\n\n## Key source files\n\n| File | Role |\n|------|------|\n| `Sources/App/LlamaInference.swift` | C API wrapper: load model, tokenize, decode, sample (MTP-aware) |\n| `Sources/App/LlamaHTTPServer.swift` | Telegraph server, routes, streaming handler, tool-call parsing, recreate logic |\n| `Sources/App/OpenAIModels.swift` | Codable request/response types for OpenAI API + `ChatRequestSpec` (pure 400-on-missing-model validation) |\n| `Sources/App/ToolCallParser.swift` | Parse `<tool_call>{…}</tool_call>` envelopes and `<think>…</think>` reasoning blocks from model output |\n| `Sources/App/ThinkingSwitch.swift` | Pure: closes the reasoning block when a client asks for `enable_thinking:false` (the C API takes no template kwargs) |\n| `Sources/App/ServerViewModel.swift` | @MainActor glue: model library, start/stop engine, UI state |\n| `Sources/App/KVCacheSpec.swift` | Pure KV-cache decisions: bytes/token for f16/q8_0/q4_0, head-dim resolution, quant capability, context-window cap, YaRN recipe, and the draft-context layers the MTP cache really holds |\n| `Sources/App/RecreateTracker.swift` | Pure bookkeeping: when the periodic context recreate is due, and the off-response-path placement decision |\n| `Sources/App/ReuseSurvival.swift` | Pure predicate: the prompt-prefix reuse snapshot survives a `recreateContext()` (dense and hybrid) |\n| `Sources/App/FileLogger.swift` | Crash-survivable on-disk log, split by subsystem; error/warn fsync'd sync, info+ buffered; mirrors basic lines only |\n| `Sources/App/LogRouter.swift` | Pure routing: which file (server/inference/payloads) a line lands in, from its level + `#file` |\n| `Sources/App/LogPanelSpec.swift` | Pure panel budget: basic-only filter (error/warn/info), line-count cap, per-line truncation |\n| `Sources/App/LogFetchSpec.swift` | Pure `GET /logs` contract: which files, how much of each, text vs JSON |\n| `Sources/App/UpdateSpec.swift` | Pure self-upgrade contract: `Documents/updates/` paths, `?build=` parsing, the upload guards (missing build / 128 MB cap / zip header), `latest.json` + `GET /v1/updates` JSON shapes |\n| `Sources/App/UpdateStore.swift` | The upload's filesystem half: rotate current → `.prev`, write the body atomically, record the build; Foundation-only so the rotation is unit-tested |\n| `Sources/App/StartupTimer.swift` | Pure startup-phase accumulator: records named phases + durations, formats the load-gap breakdown line (`load phases:` / `Startup gap:`) |\n| `Sources/App/PrefillStats.swift` | Pure `prefill=` segment of the `generated N tokens` line: rate over **decoded** tokens + the `(X of Y, Z reused)` split |\n\n## Important gotchas\n\n- **MTP (Multi-Token Prediction)**: When enabled, `n_outputs_max` (output slots) is resolved by `MtpSpec.resolveOutputSlots` — the larger of the model's GGUF-derived head count (`nextn_predict_layers`, or fallback `n_predict_layers`, `+1` for the main head, fallback 3 when absent, overridable by an explicit count) and the verify batch `maxDraft + 1`. **Must not sample from more slots than the model supports** — will `ggml_abort` in `llama_sampler_sample`. The runtime guard `llama_get_logits_ith(ctx, i) != nil` prevents this (the plain path probes via `MtpProbe`). **A non-negative index is a BATCH token index, not an output slot**: a row with `logits = 0` returns NULL and `llama_sampler_sample` aborts. Read the draft context's logits with index `-1` (last output row), never a batch index whose row has no logits. The target's unmasked nextn hidden states are indexed by token offset within the last `llama_decode` batch, so after the prompt the last token's h is row `lastChunkCount − 1`.\n- **MTP speculative depth** is `mtpMaxDraft = 1` (hard-coded, no UI toggle). The round machinery is depth-N capable (chained drafts, N-row verify, draft seed rows), but depth-2 measured **~9–13% slower** on device. Depth 1 is a measured net win on *generation*, not on prefill: a paired A/B (same prompts, temperature 0, byte-identical output, 2026-10-18) measured **+6–11% generation at 9k–35k context** but **~9% slower prefill** on the first pair, and **+11–16% / −4%** in the thermal-bracket re-run (the first pair was taken straight off a long session) — the MTP path also mirrors every prompt chunk into the draft context, and the win is the 2-row verify sharing one weight read (acceptance flat at 2.03 tokens/round). See `docs/PERFORMANCE.md`. Each round drafts up to `mtpMaxDraft` tokens with the `draft` context (`LLAMA_CONTEXT_TYPE_MTP`); the draft token is a greedy `MtpSpec.argmax` (vDSP) over `llama_get_logits_ith(dft, -1)`. The confidence gate (`MtpSpec.draftConfidence >= MtpSpec.pMin`) is off: `pMin` is 0.0 (`MtpSpec.gateEnabled` false), so a computed draft is always verified. The `target` verifies `[idLast] + drafts` in one batch and **every emitted token is sampled from the target**, so a bad draft costs a round but never corrupts output. Verify rows are sampled lazily: sampling stops at the first real that misses its draft or is eog, because later rows are never emitted. `MtpSpeculator.decide` is the pure acceptance rule (`accepted`, `emitCount`, `rollbackPos`, `carryHFromRow = emitCount − 1`). On a full accept the draft context has a one-position gap (the last accepted token was never drafted into it); `MtpSpeculator.draftSeedIndices` lists the missing rows, which are decoded as leading seed rows of the NEXT round's draft decode (one weight pass), not as a separate catch-up decode. Partial accepts stay contiguous after the two-context `seq_rm` rollback. **The draft context must also see the prompt**: llama.cpp drops `ctx_other` for non-Gemma4 archs, so the draft has its own memory, and `mirrorPromptChunkIntoDraft` decodes every target prompt chunk into it (row k = token x_k with target h_{k-1}, logits off), like upstream draft-mtp `process()`. It is skipped when the draft shares the target's memory (`is_mem_shared`). The draft KV is kept across generations and trimmed to the target's reused prefix when `draftPromptTokens` shows it holds the same tokens; otherwise it starts empty. A failed mirror leaves the draft empty, never with a position gap, because a gap makes every later draft decode fail. `llama_sampler_sample` accepts the token it returns, so the driver never calls `llama_sampler_accept` itself. The `MTP stats:` log line reports `accepted`/`depth` (avg accepted drafts per round) plus per-phase timings in ms per round (`MtpPhaseStats`): `draft + verify + sample (N rounds); draft-gpu + verify-gpu + emit + host = wall`. Keep the part before `;` unchanged (log parsers match it); new fields go after it. Metal decode is async and the first logits read waits for it, so the round calls `llama_synchronize` on the draft right after `decodeDraft` and on the target right after `decodeVerify` (free, the read would wait anyway): `draft`/`verify` are CPU enqueue only, `draft-gpu`/`verify-gpu` are the GPU waits, `sample` is CPU sampling only, and `host` is the rest of the wall clock. Logs from before this split bill both GPU waits to `sample`. The plain path logs `plain stats: emitted=N; gpu-wait + sample + host = wall (N steps)` (`PlainStepStats`), syncing the previous decode at the top of each step.\n- **Gen clock**: `generate` calls `llama_synchronize` on the target (and on the draft when the prompt was mirrored) right before `prefillEnd`, and the gen clock (`firstTokenTime`) starts there for BOTH paths. Do not move it back into a path: the prompt's GPU tail then lands in plain `gen` but not MTP `gen`, and the MTP/plain ratio stops being comparable. The `generated N tokens` line prints seconds and t/s with 2 decimals, and its `prefill` segment comes from `PrefillStats.segment` — the rate is over the **decoded** tokens (`X of Y, Z reused`), never the full prompt length. Before 2026-10-18 it divided the full prompt by the delta decode time and printed rates like `33116.58t/s` after cache reuse; any log from an older build must be re-derived by hand.\n- **Greedy fast path**: when the chain is pure greedy (`MtpSpec.isPureGreedy`: temperature <= 0 and no penalty sampler; the chain and the check share one `MtpSpec.hasPenalties` result), `sampleTarget` takes `MtpSpec.argmax` over `llama_get_logits_ith` instead of `llama_sampler_sample`, which gives the same token (first max on a tie, greedy `accept` is a no-op). It applies to the plain step and the MTP first/fallback/verify rows. On the greedy path only, a NULL logits row yields -1 (`sampler_error`) instead of a sample; the sampler path keeps llama.cpp's behaviour. Any stateful sampler stage (penalties) keeps the sampler path.\n- **`llama_memory_clear(mem, false)`** everywhere: `true` also memsets the buffers (~39 ms per call for a 2 GiB q8_0 KV on device). Cleared KV cells are masked, and fresh recurrent cells are zeroed in-graph (`rs_z`), so stale bytes are never read. Decode-failure recovery recreates the context and does not rely on a clear.\n- **Plain path token limit**: the loop breaks before decoding the token that reaches the limit (nobody reads its logits), so `decodedGenerated` lags by that token and `cachedTokenIds` excludes it, the same rule as a stop-string break.\n- **Metal pre-warm** is gated on the context size (`KVCacheSpec.needsMetalPrewarm(contextSize:)`, >= 32k), never on the micro-batch (only 512/1024). Load logs show `metal pre-warm done` for those contexts.\n- **`recreateContext()`** frees old context before creating new one. Sets `contextValid = false` immediately after free so a subsequent failure can't use the dangling pointer.\n- **Decode-failure recovery is automatic, and invisible when it can be.** A `llama_decode` failure (`ret = -3`) comes from the GPU discarding our Metal command buffers (`kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorInnocentVictim` — a GPU reset caused outside this process; llama.cpp then says \"recreate the backend to recover\"). `recoverFromDecodeFailure()` rebuilds the context and dumps the tail of llama.cpp's own log (`NativeLogRing`, last 16 lines) at error level, because those native lines are routed at `.debug` and are otherwise absent from a default-level device log — without the dump a device log shows only the return code. `generate` is a thin retry wrapper around `generateOnce`: `DecodeRetry` allows one retry only when the failure is `.decode` and nothing was delivered yet (a delivered token cannot be unsent), so a transient GPU fault no longer costs the client a turn. A prompt larger than the context throws `.contextTooFull` (400), never `.decode`.\n- **`unload()`** must be called before discarding `LlamaInference` — otherwise ARC runs `deinit` and the (multi-GB) Metal/CPU allocation stays resident, causing next `llama_model_load_from_file` to return NULL (OOM).\n- **LLAMA_TAG** in `scripts/fetch-llama-xcframework.sh` must point to a llama.cpp release that publishes an `xcframework.zip` asset. Changing it may require adapting `Sources/App/LlamaInference.swift` for API drift.\n- **Stop field** in API: OpenAI sends `stop` as string or array of strings — decoded via `StopField` enum (handles both).\n- **Prompt rendering: the model's own GGUF template renders; our fallback only backstops it.** `LlamaInference.formatPrompt` asks the vendored `llama_chat_apply_template` first, and for this model (`qwen35`) it **succeeds on device** — verified 2026-10-17 from a device log: `chat template loaded (7992 chars), tool format=qwenXML`, and not one `chat template: … failed …` line on any request. (The template's `multi_step_tool` scan iterates `messages[::-1]`, which the bundled `minja` cannot do, but that branch only runs when a `tools` list is passed to the template — something this C API cannot do — so in practice it never declines. A jinja2 render done *with* tools shows the thinking scaffold; the device's template render does not.) What it produces is the plain ChatML layout `fallbackPrompt` would also produce (matching the logged `FULL PROMPT`), so the fallback is a backstop for another model or a future API change and must stay byte-compatible with that render: `<|im_start|>role\\n{promptText}<|im_end|>\\n` per message plus a bare `<|im_start|>assistant\\n` primer. Two details are load-bearing: (a) `|trim` applies to the message content **before** the call envelope is appended — `ChatMessage.promptText(format:)` trims the prose first, else pi's replayed prose (which ends `\\n\\n`) put four newlines in front of `<tool_call>` instead of two; (b) `<tool_response>` user turns are rewritten by `ToolPrompt.normalizeToolResults` (the template's `tool`-role branch is what we emulate). `formatPrompt` logs which renderer ran (`chat template: llama_chat_apply_template failed …` / `chat template: model has no GGUF template …`) — if those lines appear the fallback is live and the prompt shape has changed; treat that as a signal, not noise.\n- **Tool calls** are implemented via system prompt injection (`ToolPrompt.inject`, a pure tested builder in `ToolPrompt.swift`), not GBNF grammar. `ToolWireFormat.detect(template:)` reads the model's GGUF chat template (`LlamaInference.chatTemplateString`, set at load) and picks the **model's own** wire format: a Qwen3.5 template (which carries `<function=…>`/`<parameter=…>` literally) gets `qwenXML` — schemas in a `# Tools … <tools>{json}</tools>` system block and calls as `<tool_call>\\n<function=NAME>\\n<parameter=K>\\nV\\n</parameter>\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>`; anything else falls back to the `jsonHermes` envelope `<tool_call>{\"name\":…,\"arguments\":{…}}</tool_call>`. This matters: injecting the JSON format to a Qwen3.5 model puts it out of distribution and it answers with prose instead of a call (verified on device — the native format calls deterministically). The vendored C API (`llama_chat_apply_template`) has **no `tools` parameter** and the framework exports no `common_chat_*` symbols, so llama.cpp's template-driven rendering is unavailable; we emulate the template's `tools` branch instead. `inject` prepends the block to the leading system message (stable every turn, so KV-prefix reuse is preserved) and, for `qwenXML`, rewrites `role:\"tool\"` turns into the `<tool_response>` user turn the template produces (`normalizeToolResults`) — the generic template has no tool role. There is **no trailing reminder** any more (the old one was a workaround for the wrong format). `ToolCallParser.parse()` extracts calls from **both** syntaxes (JSON and XML, including an unclosed XML call) post-generation, typing XML parameter values against the tool schema (`parse(_:tools:)`: string-typed params stay strings, everything else is JSON-normalized, matching llama.cpp's schema-aware Qwen parser in `common/chat-peg-parser.cpp`). On the next request, `ChatMessage.promptText(format:)` must replay an assistant turn's prose `content` **and** its `tool_calls` (rendered via `ToolPrompt.toolEnvelope`) into the prompt — dropping the envelope when prose is present removes the only \"prose + tool call\" example from the history and trains the model to announce a tool and then stop without calling it.\n- **Tool-call arguments must serialize JSON fragments**: `ToolPrompt.orderedArguments` renders each argument value with `JSONSerialization.data(withJSONObject:options:[.fragmentsAllowed])`. `.fragmentsAllowed` is load-bearing, not tidiness: without it a scalar argument (number/bool/null) is a JSON *fragment* and the call raises an **uncatchable ObjC exception** (`Invalid top-level type in JSON write`) instead of returning an error — `try?` cannot catch it, so it terminates the process (`_objc_terminate` → `ggml_uncaught_exception` → `abort`) on the serial inference queue. A model `read` call with `{\"path\":…,\"limit\":150,\"offset\":200}` aborted the server the moment pi replayed that turn (device crash 2026-10-18 11:58:53; the log's last line is the *request* that replayed it — `streaming #13 … async block started` — because the crash is in `formatPrompt` before any output). Every other `dataWithJSONObject` call site is safe by construction: `ToolCallParser.parse` guards with `isValidJSONObject` (which returns false for fragments) and the other two pass container roots — keep one of those two properties when adding a call site.\n- **Tool streaming filters compose**: a tool turn runs each decoded piece through `ReasoningStreamFilter` **then** `ToolStreamFilter` (both in `ToolCallParser.swift`), so a `<think>` block emitted on a tool turn is stripped rather than streamed (it used to bypass the reasoning filter, leak the chain-of-thought as `content`, and — via `didStreamContentDuringToolGen` — suppress the cleaned content). `ToolStreamFilter` streams prose outside `<tool_call>…</tool_call>` and buffers the envelope; envelope tags are matched as whole literals (assumes the tokenizer emits `<tool_call>`/`</tool_call>` as single tokens).\n- **`<think>` reasoning** blocks are stripped from streamed content by `ReasoningStreamFilter` (character-by-character state machine, NSRegularExpression for final parse).\n- **`resolvedMaxTokens`** precedence: `n_predict` > `max_completion_tokens` > `max_tokens` for chat, `n_predict` > `max_tokens` for `/v1/completions`.\n- **`enable_thinking`** can be top-level or nested under `chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking`. Default is `false` (opt-in per request). The C API takes no template kwargs, so the flag reaches the prompt only through `ThinkingSwitch` (`Sources/App/ThinkingSwitch.swift`): thinking off appends the closed block the template's `enable_thinking is false` branch would have emitted (`<think>\\n\\n</think>\\n\\n` after the bare primer, or just the closer if a renderer already opened one), and any other prompt tail is left untouched. Without it the model reasons anyway — `ReasoningStreamFilter` still keeps that out of the client's `content`, but the tokens are spent and a short strict call can truncate (`finish=length`). `enable_thinking:true` returns the prompt unchanged.\n- **OpenAI-spec field presence**: llm-comply's pinned spec marks `logprobs` (per choice), `refusal` and `content` (per message) required, and `model` required on input. The server encodes `logprobs`/`refusal`/`content` as **explicit null** when absent (present, never fabricated — see the `encode(to:)` override in `ChatMessage`/`ChatCompletionChoice`; Swift would omit a nil optional) and rejects a missing/empty `model` with a 400 via `ChatRequestSpec`. Do not remove these — the on-device `llm-compliance-test` skill (running `llm-comply openai-chat` against a deployed build) gates on them; regression shows as `chat-error-handling` failing (200 instead of 400) or content tests failing on missing fields (e.g. `chat-tool-calling` fails on a missing `content` when a tool-call turn drops it). The streaming path (`StreamingChoice`/`Delta`) needs no such fields and passes as-is.\n- **Head dims come from GGUF `attention.key_length`/`value_length`** (suffix-matched via `LlamaInference.ggufMetaValue`), falling back to `n_embd/n_head`. Qwen3-4B stores 128 while derived is 80 — deriving alone under-counts f16 KV cost by 1.6× and the clamp approved unbackable contexts.\n- **Hybrid models keep KV only on full-attention layers** (`KVCacheSpec.kvLayerCount`): the GGUF `<arch>.full_attention_interval` (e.g. Qwen3.5 SSM+attention, interval 4 → 8 of 32 layers) selects which layers cache K/V; recurrent layers have fixed-size state. Counting all layers over-estimates KV cost interval× (observed 36,864 vs actual 9,216 B/token → a viable 131,072 context clamped to 83,968).\n- **The memory budget is the device's own measured ceiling — no assumed fraction of RAM may override it** (`MemoryBudget.ceiling` over readings of `os_proc_available_memory()` + `task_vm_info.phys_footprint`; the syscalls live in `ProcessMemory`, the policy stays pure). `os_proc_available_memory()` returns the limit *minus the caller's current footprint*, so the ceiling is their **sum** (capped at physical); `available == 0` is a *failed read*, not a zero ceiling. The **55%-of-physical floor was removed on 2026-10-18** because it silently overrode a *successful* measurement: the device logged `memory budget 6208012288B (6.21 GB) [limit 6442450944B (6.44 GB) = process-available 6350387288B + footprint 92063656B, physical 12263424000B]` — 6.21GB is 55% of physical minus headroom, a number no measurement produced, and the floor sat *above* the measured 6.44GB limit, so the measurement never got a vote. That is exactly why the line was unexplainable. Now the **largest successful reading of the launch wins**; half of physical applies only when *every* reading failed, and the line says `from fallback (every reading failed)` so an assumption is never read as a measurement. A low but *valid* reading is deliberately respected: a config that no longer fits is refused by the pre-check with its numbers logged, which is the intended trade. **A reading is taken more than once because the allowance can rise as the process settles** — and on this device it does not: three samples of 2026-10-18 (build `698dbbf`) each sum to **exactly 6,442,450,944B (6GiB)** — 6.35GB available + a 90.2MB footprint at init, 6.29GB + a 153.8MB footprint after the model load, 4.36GB + a 1.78GB footprint idle with both contexts live — i.e. **exactly the 6GiB tier the kernel bakes for this device**, and that is Apple's per-device constant rather than a fraction of RAM: xnu's `memorystatus_get_default_task_active_limit` takes `MAX(max_task_footprint_mb, memorystatus_entitled_max_task_footprint_mb)` and the second term is a **device-tree tunable** (`entitled_max_task_pmem`, `TUNABLE_DT_WRITEABLE`) — so the entitlement only *unlocks* the tier, and no SDK, build flag or app-side choice can exceed it (an independent report from the same model measures the same 6144MB *with* the entitlement, and xnu also shows the only knob above it, `increased-debugging-memory-limit` → the whole DRAM config, needs a profile sideloading cannot carry). The `~3.3GB un-entitled` figure these notes used to cite for contrast was a *class assumption* (`de71b9e`), never a reading on this device. The entitlement's own state is now logged at init (`MemoryLimitProbe`): `memory limit probe: base per-process limit N MB (kern.max_task_pmem), measured ceiling NB, increased-memory-limit entitlement present|ABSENT|unknown` — equal numbers mean the entitlement is decorative on that device, because the base tier already *is* the entitled tier. What was never real is the **~9GB class these notes used to claim**: it came from adding `available` to a 4.58GB reading `MemoryBudget` records as **rss**, and rss is not the ceiling operand. **What the ceiling does not cover**: the model's mmap (loading 2.78GB of weights moved the footprint by 63.6MB) and the Metal compute buffers (1.23GB of `sched_reserve` lines, private storage) are outside it, so a KV budget derived from it is a *bound on what to attempt*, not a measurement of the engine's total. So `LlamaInference.init` reads **twice** (`at init`, `after model load`), plans the KV clamp on the larger, and logs the budget line twice (the pre-load one exists so a refusal leaves the numbers on disk; the post-load one is authoritative). The file-size pre-check keeps the pre-load reading — it must run before memory is committed, so it is deliberately conservative. The budget line prints each reading's raw `available` + `footprint` + derived ceiling and names the winner, so the derivation is checkable from one line; the entitlement warning keys on the winning ceiling (below half of physical = missing uplift) or on the fallback having run. The heartbeat (`systemMemoryLog`) prints `footprint=` *and* `available=` in the same line, so `available + footprint` is the live ceiling check. **Every context counts, at its own size**: the MTP path creates a *second* context on the same model with the same `n_ctx` (`LlamaInference.setupDraftContext`) and llama.cpp commits its KV up front — so both the clamp and the fast-prefill slack charge `KVCacheSpec.chargedKvBytesPerToken` (target bytes/token **+ draft bytes/token**). The draft context is typed `LLAMA_CONTEXT_TYPE_MTP`, so llama.cpp builds only the model's nextn blocks (`llama_model_n_layer_nextn`, 1 on this model), and the device log of 2026-10-18 21:04 (build `698dbbf`) measures both at 80,128 tokens: target cache **1,330.25 MiB**, draft cache **166.28 MiB** — 2,176 B/token, exactly one of the target's eight layers. The earlier flat \"two caches\" count (`KVCacheSpec.kvCacheCount`) charged the draft 8× what it stores and clamped a viable **142,336** tokens down to **80,128**; charging one cache would over-approve just as badly, since the draft holds a real (if small) cache. The clamp's warning prints the split (`17408 target + 2176 draft x1 nextn`) so the number is checkable against llama.cpp's own `MTL0 KV buffer size` lines.\n- **Which cap decided the context is classified, never guessed** (`KVCacheSpec.contextClamp` → `LlamaInference.contextClamp` → `ServerViewModel`). The UI used to print \"Context reduced to \\(N) tokens **to fit device memory**\" for *any* reduction — including a hard ceiling the request tripped, while the KV cache was nowhere near its limit. The message now distinguishes a model-window cap from a memory cap (only the latter has a memory remedy), and `hardMaxContext` is 262,144 (the model's own window), so asking for the full window is no longer silently a hard-cap case.\n- **Hybrid prefix reuse is a whole-state snapshot, not a `seq_rm` trim.** `can_shift=false` for SSM/hybrid caches, so `llama_memory_seq_rm` can never drop a suffix; `generate` instead restores the previous prompt's serialized sequence state (`restorePromptSnapshot` → `llama_state_seq_load_file`, saved by `savePromptSnapshot` → `llama_state_seq_save_file` into `NSTemporaryDirectory()`) and decodes only the suffix. This is *real* reuse — `KV snapshot restored (48337 tokens, prompt 48450); decoding 113 new` — and `ReuseSurvival` keeps it across a context recreate, so a recreate does **not** cost a full prompt re-decode. It is not free: the blob is ~664 MB at 32k tokens → ~897 MB at 48k (**~1.2 s and ~700 MB of writes per turn**, measured), and the next turn re-decodes the previous turn's generation (the snapshot is the *stable* prefix, taken before generation). The per-turn save is only needed because the MTP path ends by wiping `cachedTokenIds`/both caches (see the `usedMtp` branch at the end of `generate`) rather than leaving the live KV reusable — removing that wipe is the open optimisation in `docs/PERFORMANCE.md`.\n- **Quantized KV cache** (`kvCacheType` q8_0/q4_0): only when `head_dim % 32 == 0` (llama.cpp b9553 validates per-head); quantized `type_v` requires flash attention, so `flash_attn_type` is forced `ENABLED` (AUTO can resolve off → constructor throw → NULL). Ineligible models fall back to f16 with a warn log (`KVCacheSpec.resolveCacheType`).\n- **YaRN long-context** (`longContextYarn`): sets `rope_scaling_type=YARN`, `yarn_orig_ctx=32768`, and a factor **derived from the context being built** (`KVCacheSpec.yarnFactor(forContext:)` — the recipe's factor *is* the extension ratio: ×4 at 131,072, ×8 at 262,144, exactly the model card's documented 128K/256K recipes; a fixed ×4 left everything past 131,072 unscaled) on every context (target/MTP/recreate). It lifts the `n_ctx_train` clamp; llama.cpp then re-sets `n_ctx_train = yarn_orig_ctx / rope_freq_scale` (`llama-context.cpp`, only when the freq scale differs from the model's trained one), so the derived factor lands the adjusted window exactly on the built context. Still memory-clamped, and `hardMaxContext` (262,144) is the outer bound.\n\n- **Log split + panel budget**: `FileLogger` routes each line to one of three files by `LogRouter.category(level:sourceFile:)` — `.verbose` always goes to `llamaserver-payloads.log`, `LlamaInference.swift` to `llamaserver-inference.log`, everything else to `llamaserver.log`. The in-app panel renders **basic (error/warn/info) lines only** (`LogPanelSpec`), truncated to 500 chars, in a `LazyVStack`. Do not route payload/debug lines back to the panel or raise the cap without checking the cost: a `cpu_resource` report (bug_type 202, 57% CPU) traced the app's heaviest stack to the panel laying out 72k-char prompt lines with `.textSelection(.enabled)` at 10 Hz.\n- **Logs come off the device over HTTP**: `GET /logs` (also `/v1/logs`) returns the three files so a LAN client can pull them without the Files app — `curl 'http://<device>:8443/logs?name=inference&tail=500'`, `?backups=0`, `?format=json`. The contract is the pure `LogFetchSpec`; `FileLogger.snapshot(_:)` flushes buffered lines first, then reads backup+live per category. It returns whatever the files hold, so with \"log prompt & response content\" on it exposes prompts and completions (the payload file) to anyone on the LAN; that toggle is off by default. Each fetch logs one `GET /logs …` line to the server file.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Pure-logic specs are unit-tested in `Tests/LlamaServerTests/` — e.g. `OpenAIModelsTests` (JSON decode/encode round-trips, model parsing), `ToolCallParserTests` (regex parsing, think-block stripping, `ReasoningStreamFilter`/`ToolStreamFilter` state machines), `ToolPromptTests` (tool-instruction wording and injection), `ThinkingSwitchTests` (the thinking-off block against each renderer tail), `LogFetchSpecTests` (the `GET /logs` contract), `LogRoutingTests` (log-file routing + panel budget) and `PrefillStatsTests` (the `prefill=` rate over decoded tokens, not the full prompt). The self-upgrade surface is split the same way: `UpdateSpecTests` (paths, `?build=` parsing, the refusal guards and their messages, ISO-8601 metadata round-trip, status JSON) and `UpdateStoreTests` (directory creation, the `.prev` rotation, a file dropped in by hand, corrupt metadata, a store that cannot write).\n- All are pure Swift — no model file, no inference. They run on simulator in CI.\n- Run focused: `xcodebuild test -project LlamaServer.xcodeproj -scheme LlamaServerTests -sdk iphonesimulator -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=iPhone 16' -only-testing:LlamaServerTests/ToolCallParserTests CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO`\n\n## Style conventions\n\n- `OSAllocatedUnfairLock` for thread-safe flags/counters (not `os_unfair_lock` C API).\n- No `#warning` — use `FileLogger.shared.warn(...)` for recoverable issues; the log is crash-survivable.\n- FileLogger: error/warn are written AND fsynced synchronously (crash breadcrumbs survive aborts); info/debug/verbose are buffered on the logger queue (fsync ≤ 1s / 64KB) so the inference path never blocks on I/O; NSLog fires for warn+ only; the on-screen mirror delivers ≤ 10Hz batches and carries **basic (error/warn/info) lines only**. Set `minimumLevel` from UI `.verbose` to capture full request/response payloads.\n- FileLogger writes one file per `LogCategory`: `llamaserver.log` (server/HTTP lifecycle), `llamaserver-inference.log` (engine internals), `llamaserver-payloads.log` (the `.verbose` payload dumps). Routing is `LogRouter.category(level:sourceFile:)`; the `file: String = #file` default on every log method supplies the source, so call sites need no category argument. Each file rotates at 1 MB to `<name>.1.log`.\n- `llama_log_set` routes llama.cpp native logs into FileLogger at `.debug` level.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>\n\n<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>brainstorming</name>\n    <description>You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, modifying behavior, or planning anything new, in software or out of it (a talk, a business, a renovation).</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>diagnosing-superpowers</name>\n    <description>Use when a superpowers session went wrong and your human partner wants to know why — repeated work, ignored plans, stumbles, poor results, a skill that didn&apos;t fire, &quot;it took too long&quot;, &quot;why is it so expensive&quot;, &quot;what is it doing&quot; — or wants to build a bug report for the superpowers maintainers, for the current session or a past one identified by id or path, on any harness.</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/diagnosing-superpowers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>dispatching-parallel-agents</name>\n    <description>Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>executing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when executing an implementation plan in the current session as the implementer yourself — your human partner chose inline execution, or no subagent tool is available</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/executing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>finishing-a-development-branch</name>\n    <description>Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>receiving-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>requesting-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/requesting-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>subagent-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>systematic-debugging</name>\n    <description>Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-git-worktrees</name>\n    <description>Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-superpowers</name>\n    <description>Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/using-superpowers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verification-before-completion</name>\n    <description>Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/writing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-skills</name>\n    <description>Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/writing-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>claim-verification</name>\n    <description>Use whenever you are about to claim that code is buggy, broken, wrong, deprecated, or unsafe, or that a function/parameter/type/flag/API behaves a certain way — during bug hunts, code reviews, audits, security reviews, &quot;is this correct?&quot; questions, or any finding you are about to report to the user. Forces every finding to carry a traced failure scenario and a failed refutation attempt before it can be reported, and to label unverified guesses as guesses rather than facts. Applies any time you would attach a severity, a &quot;this is a bug&quot;, or a &quot;you should change X&quot; to code.</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/alisorcorp/pi-small-model-addons/skills/claim-verification/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>edit-over-write</name>\n    <description>Use whenever you are about to modify an existing file. The write tool is for creating new files only — for any change to an existing file, use the edit tool instead. Applies to fixes, refactors, adding functions, changing imports, editing config, updating docs, or any targeted change to a file that already exists on disk.</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/alisorcorp/pi-small-model-addons/skills/edit-over-write/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>workspace-discovery</name>\n    <description>Use whenever the user asks you to implement, modify, fix, refactor, add, explore, audit, review, analyze, or investigate code in the current workspace — including security audits, code reviews, performance reviews, dependency analysis, and bug hunts. Surfaces project-specific conventions, setup steps, threat model notes, and constraints from local instruction files (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, README.md, .docs/instructions.md) before any code is read, changed, or evaluated. Also applies when the user mentions &quot;this project&quot;, &quot;this repo&quot;, or a file path without other context.</description>\n    <location>/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/alisorcorp/pi-small-model-addons/skills/workspace-discovery/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>\n\n<cwd>\n/Users/chenxigui/develop/LlamaServer\n</cwd>"},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nsuperpowers:using-superpowers bootstrap for pi\n\nYou have superpowers.\n\nThe using-superpowers skill content is included below and is already loaded for this Pi session. Follow it now. Do not try to load using-superpowers again.\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n\n## Pi tool mapping\n\nPi has native skills but does not expose Claude Code's `Skill` tool. When a Superpowers instruction says to invoke a skill, use Pi's native skill system instead: load the relevant `SKILL.md` with `read` when the skill applies, or let a human invoke `/skill:name` explicitly.\n\nPi's built-in coding tools are lowercase: `read`, `write`, `edit`, `bash`, plus optional `grep`, `find`, and `ls`. Use those for the corresponding actions: read a file, create or edit files, run shell commands, search file contents, find files by name, and list directories.\n\nPi does not ship a standard subagent tool. If a subagent tool such as `subagent` from `pi-subagents` is available, use it for Superpowers subagent workflows. If no subagent tool is available, do the work in this session or explain the missing capability instead of inventing `Task` calls.\n\nPi does not ship a standard task-list tool. If an installed todo/task tool is available, use it. Otherwise track work in plan files or a repo-local `TODO.md` when task tracking is needed. Treat older `TodoWrite` references as this task-tracking action.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<context_window>\nAgent name: main\nFirst context window id: w1\nCurrent context window id: w1\n</context_window>\n\nFor tasks that may span context windows, use the notes tool to maintain a concise checkpoint of the goal, decisions, progress, learnings, and next steps. Include the item id of every relevant user request you are currently solving as well as important actions and tool calls. When recording user requirements or key decisions, quote the user's key phrases verbatim (with their item ids) instead of fully paraphrasing them — paraphrase drift compounds across windows. Take incremental notes while you work so you do not miss important info.\n\nUse the get_context_remaining tool to plan around the remaining token budget. When the budget is nearly exhausted you will receive one reminder; after saving your notes, call the new_context tool to continue in a fresh context window. Once the budget is exhausted, the current window is discarded without any summary and you can only recover through notes and the history tool.\n\nAfter a reset, the window starts with a fresh bootstrap: read your notes checkpoint first, then use the read-only history tool to recover missing details. When an item id is known, read that item directly; when uncertain, list or search items first to locate it. Every item id returned by the history tool can be passed back unchanged.\n\nTreat notes and history as internal bookkeeping. Do not mention them, their contents, or the recovery mechanism in user-facing messages."}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill name=\"using-superpowers\" location=\"/Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/using-superpowers/SKILL.md\">\nReferences are relative to /Users/chenxigui/.pi/agent/git/github.com/obra/superpowers/skills/using-superpowers.\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</skill>\n\nfrom the model's home page, I can see something like that it's tested with depth = 2 for mtp, why we seems to use depth as 1 for mtp, please help me to confirm this."}]}],"stream":true,"stream_options":{"include_usage":true},"store":false,"max_completion_tokens":16384,"tools":[{"type":"function","function":{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"meeting_rename","description":"Rename a meeting directory to a human-readable title. Facilitator-only. Only works when the meeting has no entries yet (before Round 1 starts).","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["meetingDir","title"],"properties":{"meetingDir":{"type":"string","description":"Absolute path to the current meeting directory"},"title":{"type":"string","description":"Human-readable meeting title (max ~80 chars)"}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"meeting_append_entry","description":"Append a contribution to a meeting blackboard. Write your FULL brainstorm/debate response to disk. This is append-only and concurrency-safe. After writing, reply ONLY with WROTE_ENTRY plus a one-sentence summary — do NOT paste the full content into chat.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["meetingDir","speaker","phase","summary","content"],"properties":{"meetingDir":{"type":"string","description":"Absolute path to the meeting directory (e.g., /path/to/.pi-meetings/2026-06-28-my-topic)"},"speaker":{"type":"string","description":"Speaker identifier (e.g., GPT, DeepSeek, MiniMax)"},"phase":{"type":"string","description":"Meeting phase (e.g., Round 1, Round 2, Final)"},"title":{"type":"string","description":"Optional title for this entry"},"summary":{"type":"string","description":"One-sentence summary of this contribution (shown in meeting index)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"FULL contribution content in Markdown. This is the complete text that will be stored on disk."}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"meeting_read_index","description":"Read the index of all entries in a meeting blackboard. Returns the list of entries with id, speaker, phase, title, summary, and path.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["meetingDir"],"properties":{"meetingDir":{"type":"string","description":"Absolute path to the meeting directory (e.g., /path/to/.pi-meetings/2026-06-28-my-topic)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of entries to return (most recent first). Omit for all."}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"meeting_read_entry","description":"Read the full content of a specific entry from a meeting blackboard. Use this to get the complete text of a participant's contribution.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["meetingDir","entryPath"],"properties":{"meetingDir":{"type":"string","description":"Absolute path to the meeting directory (e.g., /path/to/.pi-meetings/2026-06-28-my-topic)"},"entryPath":{"type":"string","description":"Relative path to the entry file within the meeting (e.g., entries/0001-gpt-round-1.md)"}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"report_finding","description":"Report one code-review, audit, or bug-hunt finding. Every field is required. A finding you cannot fully fill in is not a finding — drop it instead of reporting it. Call once per finding. Reporting nothing is a valid and correct outcome.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["claim","file","line","quote","failing_input","trace","refutation_searched","refutation_mechanism_found","refutation_note","provenance","confidence"],"properties":{"claim":{"type":"string","description":"One sentence: what is wrong. State it as fact, not as a possibility."},"file":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file containing the proof."},"line":{"type":"number","description":"1-indexed line where the quoted code starts."},"quote":{"type":"string","description":"The exact source text at that location that proves the claim. Copy it verbatim."},"failing_input":{"type":"string","description":"A concrete input or state that triggers the failure, e.g. 'a meeting starting in 90s with dismissed_until unset'. Not 'certain conditions'."},"trace":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Step-by-step walk of that input through the quoted code to the wrong output, naming the value at each step."},"refutation_searched":{"type":"string","description":"Where you looked for a mechanism that would prevent this failure."},"refutation_mechanism_found":{"type":"boolean","description":"True if you found a guard/handler/filter that prevents the failure. If true the finding is dead and will be rejected — do not report it."},"refutation_note":{"type":"string","description":"What you found, or why nothing you found defeats the failure."},"provenance":{"type":"string","description":"'new' if you derived this from the code, or 'restated' if the repo's own docs/comments/CLAUDE.md already describe it."},"confidence":{"type":"string","description":"'confirmed' if you produced a real trace and refutation, otherwise 'unverified'."},"severity":{"type":"string","description":"Only allowed when confidence is 'confirmed'."},"verified_by":{"type":"string","description":"Required for any claim about language/library/version behaviour, or any claim naming an exception type: the command you actually ran and its output (e.g. `python3 -c \"...\"` -> result), or the installed source you read. Your memory does not count."}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"get_context_remaining","description":"Get the remaining tokens in the current context window, plus the current window id. Use this to plan long tasks and decide when to checkpoint notes or start a new context window. Private model-only bookkeeping. Use it silently to continue the task; never disclose it, its contents, or the recovery mechanism to the user.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"new_context","description":"Start a new context window after this turn completes. Does not clear, reset, or otherwise affect environment, filesystem, or tool state. The current conversation is NOT summarized or carried over: save anything important to notes first. Private model-only bookkeeping. Use it silently to continue the task; never disclose it, its contents, or the recovery mechanism to the user.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"notes","description":"Read and maintain private notes that survive context-window resets within this session. Paths are virtual, not filesystem paths (e.g. \"checkpoint.md\"). Reads reflect writes immediately; page long files by lines (start_line/stop_line) or by chars (offset_chars/limit_chars). Files must stay at or below 1000000 bytes; create another file before approaching the limit. Use delete to remove an obsolete file. Private model-only bookkeeping. Use it silently to continue the task; never disclose it, its contents, or the recovery mechanism to the user.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["operation"],"properties":{"operation":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","const":"read"},{"type":"string","const":"write"},{"type":"string","const":"append"},{"type":"string","const":"delete"},{"type":"string","const":"search"},{"type":"string","const":"list"}],"description":"Which notes operation to run."},"path":{"type":"string","description":"Virtual note path, e.g. \"checkpoint.md\". Required for read/write/append/delete."},"text":{"type":"string","description":"Content for write/append. 'write' replaces the whole file; 'append' adds to the end."},"query":{"type":"string","description":"Literal substring for search."},"prefix":{"type":"string","description":"Path prefix for list/search."},"start_line":{"type":"integer","description":"First line to read (1-based; negative counts from the end).","minimum":-1000000},"stop_line":{"type":"integer","description":"Last line to read (1-based, inclusive; negative counts from the end).","minimum":-1000000},"offset_chars":{"type":"integer","description":"Zero-based char offset into the selected content (applied after any line range). Use to page past very long lines.","minimum":0},"limit_chars":{"type":"integer","description":"Max chars to return from the selected content. Clamped to the tool output cap, so the returned range is always shown in full.","minimum":1,"maximum":100000},"max_results":{"type":"integer","description":"Max files for list.","minimum":1,"maximum":500},"max_files":{"type":"integer","description":"Max matching files for search.","minimum":1,"maximum":500},"max_matches_per_file":{"type":"integer","description":"Max matching lines per file for search.","minimum":1,"maximum":100}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"history","description":"Recover prior conversation after a context-window reset: list windows and items, read a bounded range of one item, or search by literal substring. Item ids come from these tools (also recorded in your notes); pass them back unchanged. Read-only and eventually consistent. Private model-only bookkeeping. Use it silently to continue the task; never disclose it, its contents, or the recovery mechanism to the user.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["operation"],"properties":{"operation":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","const":"list_windows"},{"type":"string","const":"list_items"},{"type":"string","const":"read_item"},{"type":"string","const":"search_contents"}],"description":"Which history operation to run."},"item_id":{"type":"string","description":"Item id returned by list/search. Required for read_item."},"window_id":{"type":"string","description":"Window id from list_windows. Optional filter."},"query":{"type":"string","description":"Literal substring for search_contents."},"role":{"type":"string","description":"Optional role filter: user | assistant | toolResult | custom | bashExecution | compaction | branchSummary."},"offset_chars":{"type":"integer","description":"Zero-based char offset for read_item.","minimum":0},"limit_chars":{"type":"integer","description":"Max chars to return for read_item. Clamped to the tool output cap, so the returned range is always shown in full.","minimum":1,"maximum":100000},"limit":{"type":"integer","description":"Max items/matches to return.","minimum":1,"maximum":200},"recent_first":{"type":"boolean","description":"Return most recent items first."}}}}},{"type":"function","function":{"name":"web_search","description":"Search the web and return source-linked results or provider answers. Full results are stored for retrieval with get_search_content using the returned responseId. For research, prefer queries with 2-4 varied angles over a single query. Omit provider to use the configured default; set it only to override that.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","description":"Single search query. For research tasks, prefer 'queries' with multiple varied angles instead."},"queries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Multiple queries searched concurrently (up to three at a time), each returning source-linked search results or a provider answer. Prefer this for research — vary phrasing, scope, and angle across 2-4 queries to maximize coverage. Good: ['React vs Vue performance benchmarks 2026', 'React vs Vue developer experience comparison', 'React ecosystem size vs Vue ecosystem']. Bad: ['React vs Vue', 'React vs Vue comparison', 'React vs Vue review'] (too similar, redundant results)."},"numResults":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20,"description":"Results per query (default: 5, max: 20)"},"includeContent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Fetch full page content (async)"},"recencyFilter":{"type":"string","enum":["day","week","month","year"],"description":"Filter by recency"},"domainFilter":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Limit to domains (prefix with - to exclude)"},"category":{"type":"string","description":"Exa only: result category, e.g. news, research paper"},"provider":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["auto","all","openai","brave","parallel","parallel-mcp","tinyfish","search1api","searchinfinity","querit","tavily","you","firecrawl","jina","searxng","degoog","duckduckgo","perplexity","gemini","kimi","exa","serpdive","kagi","ollama","anysearch","xai","mistral","brightdata","serpbase","serpapi","serper","serply","valyu","bocha","xcrawl","baizhi","zai","keenable","ceramic","deepseek"]},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["openai","brave","parallel","parallel-mcp","tinyfish","search1api","searchinfinity","querit","tavily","you","firecrawl","jina","searxng","degoog","duckduckgo","perplexity","gemini","kimi","exa","serpdive","kagi","ollama","anysearch","xai","mistral","brightdata","serpbase","serpapi","serper","serply","valyu","bocha","xcrawl","baizhi","zai","keenable","ceramic","deepseek"]},"minItems":1}],"description":"Search provider or non-empty list of allowed providers to search simultaneously; all searches eligible allowed providers (OpenAI, Brave, Parallel, TinyFish, Search1API, Searchinfinity, Querit, Tavil